{
  "dom_id": "step-q38252",
  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "Tom's spherical basketball has a hole in it. Its volume decreases at a rate that's proportional to its surface area. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ of the ball. Assume that $k>0.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*Its **volume decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to its **surface area**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a sphere* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional to its surface area $S.$* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kS,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$ Now, using the fact that $S=4\\pi {r}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-4\\pi k{r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a sphere is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi k{r}^{2} \\\\ 4\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi k{r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{4\\pi k{r}^{2}}{4\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
